### 1. Add explicit section-boundary assertions
**File:** `tests/unit/flow/skill-prelude-auto.test.js`  
**Issue:** The test slices with `text.indexOf(...)` directly. If `"### B. Prelude"` or `"### A. Entry"` is missing, `indexOf` returns `-1`, and the slice can still produce misleading text instead of failing clearly.  
**Suggestion:** Store both indexes, assert they are present and ordered, then slice:
```js
const start = text.indexOf("### A. Entry");
const end = text.indexOf("### B. Prelude");

assert.notStrictEqual(start, -1);
assert.notStrictEqual(end, -1);
assert.ok(start < end);

const entry = text.slice(start, end);
```

### 2. Use table-driven assertions for expected and forbidden wording
**File:** `tests/unit/flow/skill-prelude-auto.test.js`  
**Issue:** The new test repeats several `assert.match` / `assert.doesNotMatch` calls with hard-coded prose patterns, which will get noisier as wording requirements grow.  
**Suggestion:** Group patterns into `forbiddenPatterns` and `requiredPatterns` arrays and iterate over them. This keeps the test easier to extend while preserving the same coverage.

